  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead blew up in the ninth. Milwaukee scored twice, and the Padres never answered.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what one-run deficits look like when runners don't move. They left nine men on base across the game and scored on only two of those chances. Which meant they spent the night handing Milwaukee opportunities to steal one. Michael King was clean for five innings, the bullpen held until Mason Miller couldn't, and then the door closed. The math is simple: they got the starter they needed and the defense stayed intact, but the offense left the winning run on base three separate times when the game still mattered. Ty France drove in both runs the team managed, which was enough to lead through eight. It was not enough to keep it. This is how close games become losses. Not through collapse, but through waste.",
    "what_to_watch": "Dustin May takes the mound tonight for Milwaukee at 6:30 PT, carrying a 4.70 ERA into a ballpark where every run carries weight. Nick Pivetta has thrown to a 1.48 ERA over his last four starts and gets first crack at a repeat matchup that the Padres need to win, not just play close.",
